**Read:** [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), experimental kinetics, solution reactions, and catalysis; [Química geral no laboratório — Marcela Boroski, organizer](../sources/Quimica-geral-no-laboratorio.lingua#r_48YEN2SA34DW0FPCSTMVTW3CTD), the complete kinetics activity and its discussion; [Analytical Chemistry 2.1 — David Harvey](../sources/Analytical-Chemistry-Harvey.lingua#r_0XMY75E3FRXRGR3BK28V3JAC0T), chapter 13’s observations and method conditions.

A measured overall rate can combine reaction, transport, phase change, instrument response, and sampling. Stirring, particle size, geometry, temperature gradients, or gas/liquid transfer can change the observation without changing the underlying elementary chemistry. Define where a concentration or temperature is measured relative to the active region.

Compare characteristic times for reaction and relevant transport under stated models. A dimensionless ratio can organize limiting regimes, but its definition and length/velocity scales must be explicit. Similar observed behavior under one condition does not uniquely separate contributions. A chemical-engineering treatment will deepen reaction–transport coupling.

For a dissolving/reacting solid, increased surface area can affect transfer and available reaction sites. For a catalyst, pores can introduce internal gradients. For calorimetry, heat release and thermometer response can distort a transient. Preserve these physical alternatives before assigning an observed rate change to a new chemical mechanism.

**Work:** design a paper or appropriately supervised study varying one controlled transport influence while keeping composition and temperature information adequate. Predict what an intrinsic-rate model and a transport-limited model would each imply. Identify sampling and instrument-response checks required to interpret the comparison.

Read the experimental-method and heterogeneous-catalysis discussions alongside the laboratory activity. Use this Record to connect chemistry with fluids, heat transfer, surfaces, and measurement rather than requiring completion of every engineering subject before asking a useful question. Keep the later specialist work pending until its unique theory and practical methods are developed.

